From 420440f689ea0a1f9243e2074bbd0bf065919786 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 16:15:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 74/83] elfobj/readelf: fix OOB reads on hostile ELF/DWARF
 (audit)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On i386 (32-bit size_t) `off + len` and `count * entsize` wrap at 2^32,
bypassing bounds checks when parsing untrusted ELF.  Route every such
computation through the existing overflow-checked helpers (elf__u64_add /
elf__u64_mul / elf__bounds_ok, and the non-wrapping `off > size ||
size - off < N` form in readelf), and require an in-bounds NUL before
treating any string-table offset as a C string.

elfobj library:
- elf_gnu_ext.c: the note parser (elf_note_count/elf_note_at) computed
  nstart+namesz / dstart+descsz in 32-bit, so namesz/descsz near 0xFFFFFFFF
  wrapped past every check — OOB read / unterminated strcmp / non-terminating
  loop, reachable from readelf -n / build-id / GNU-property.  Rewritten with
  64-bit checked spans, forward-progress, and a memchr NUL guard.
- elf_validate.c / elf_link.c: the GNU-property note walkers (copies of the
  hardened gnu_ext readers) had the same 32-bit wrap — fixed with elf__u64_add
  and per-field bounds; got/plt entry-count multiplies use elf__u64_mul.
- elf_read.c reloc width, elf_reloc.c builder offset, elf_write.c NULL-segment
  deref + verdef/group/strtab allocation overflows, elf_dwarf.c non-wrapping
  span check.

readelf (its own hand-rolled parser): view_u16/u32/u64 bounds, shoff/phoff +
entsize*index 64-bit recompute, SYSV .hash and GNU .gnu.hash histogram bounds,
and a strtab_str() in-bounds-NUL guard for every section-name/symbol/dynamic/
version/PT_INTERP/note/NT_FILE/ARM-attribute string deref.

All OOB reads (info leak / crash); no OOB write (elfedit rebuilds the object
rather than patching attacker offsets).  elfobj native test suite: no
regressions vs pristine HEAD (the 2 remaining failures pre-date this), and
test_roundtrip now passes (a malformed test object was corrected).

Found by a security audit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
